    "result_text": "All tests pass and the build succeeds. Here's what I implemented.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec: **a new owner's `membershipLevel` cannot exceed one above the current maximum among their household members; return the capped value; no cap when there are no existing household members.**\n\n`membershipLevel` was previously *derived on the fly* at response-mapping time (`MembershipLevel.of(owner)`) and never stored, so there was nothing to cap. I made it a persisted, capped value computed during owner creation:\n\n1. **`Owner` entity** \u2014 added a persisted `Integer membershipLevel` field (`membership_level` column) with getter/setter, and added the column to all four DB schemas (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`).\n\n2. **New pipeline step `CapOwnerMembershipLevel`** \u2014 computes the uncapped level via `MembershipLevel.of(owner)`, finds existing household members (same `householdId`, matched exactly as `AssignOwnerHousehold` does), takes their maximum level (using each member's stored/already-capped level when present), and caps the new owner at `max + 1`. With no existing member, no cap applies. The result is stamped on the owner and persisted by `SaveOwner`.\n\n3. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired `capMembershipLevel` in after `dedupeCustomerCode` and before `save` (under transaction governance), so household id, size and namesake count are all set before the level is computed.\n\n4. **`OwnerMapper`** \u2014 `membershipLevel` now returns the stored value when present, falling back to the derived `MembershipLevel.of(owner)` for records without one (e.g. seed data).\n\n## One change beyond the literal spec\n\nThe new test creates two owners in the **same household** (same last name + postcode) with different telephones/emails and expects *both* to be created. `CheckOwnerIdentityUnique` was rejecting any second owner sharing a household id with a 409 \u2014 which contradicted `OwnerIdentity`'s own documentation (\"two members of the same household with different telephones have different keys and are both allowed\"). I aligned the check with that documented design: it now rejects only when the **whole** `OwnerIdentity.key` (telephone\uff5cemail\uff5chousehold) matches an existing non-deleted owner, keeping the `sharesHousehold` bypass. Without this, the test's second create could never succeed. There were no other tests exercising the old household-only rejection.\n\nFull suite: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**. The audit log confirms the two household owners are created with levels 1 and 2 (2 \u2264 1 + 1).",
